# Project rules

## Input and controls
- Every input action must be rebindable from a settings screen. Gameplay code queries
  actions by name only (`Input.is_action_pressed("attack")`) and never reads raw
  keycodes or mouse buttons, so rebinding is purely an InputMap concern.
- Control vocabulary (all InputMap actions): move_up, move_down, move_left, move_right,
  run (hold to sprint), jump, dash (has a cooldown), attack, parry, interact.
- Default bindings: move = WASD + arrow keys, run = Shift, jump = Space, dash = Q,
  attack = left mouse, parry = right mouse, interact = E, settings = Tab; joypad
  A/B/X for jump/run/dash, RB attack, LB parry. Gamepad movement is the LEFT STICK
  (axis 0 for left/right, axis 1 for up/down) - never face buttons, which stay on
  actions. Interact is E only - Space is jump and must never double as interact, so
  jumping next to an NPC does not start dialogue.
- Input bindings are stored PER SAVE SLOT like every other setting, in the slot's
  `[bindings]` section (res://scripts/save_game.gd, written through InputRemap's
  set_bindings / get_bindings). Rebinding a key in one playthrough never changes
  another, and New Game starts from the project defaults because a fresh slot
  records none. SaveGame emits `active_slot_changed` on New Game and Load, which is
  what makes InputRemap re-read the bindings. Never write a global bindings file.

## Structure
- One entity per file: player, npcs, animals, monsters and props each live under
  res://entities/<type>/<name>.tscn + .gd. Buildings and reusable architecture live
  under res://structures/. Shared logic lives under res://scripts/. UI under res://ui/.
- Prefer one reusable scene instanced many times with exported tunables over duplicated
  scenes (one cottage.tscn for every cottage, one fence.tscn for every fence run).
- res://main.tscn stays a thin entry point that only instances res://scenes/village.tscn.

## Rendering
- Structures that can hide the player fade via res://shaders/occluder_fade.gdshader,
  driven by res://scripts/occluder_fader.gd. The fade must stay a GPU-side screen-space
  circle with one uniform write per moved frame - no per-object scripts, traversal or raycasts.

## World orientation
- The village is authored north-up: -Z is north (the gate, screen-up), +Z is south (toward
  the camera), +X is east, -X is west. The gate stands on the north edge of the village.
- The world was turned 90 degrees left by rotating the groups (Structures, Dressing,
  Checkpoints, plus the Lane and Fields meshes) rather than re-authoring every node, so a
  transform written inside one of those groups is in that group's pre-turn frame: local +X
  points at where the gate used to be (east), which is now world north. Do not hand-rotate a
  new structure to compensate - place it and it turns with the group.
- The fence run names (FenceN01, FenceE03, ...) date from the pre-turn layout and no longer
  describe the side they sit on.

## Version control
- The agent stages and commits each finished change itself, with a short descriptive
  message, and leaves it at that. The user owns the remote and does every push: never run
  git push, pull, checkout or discard. Reading git state (status, log, diff) is fine.

## Settings and persistence
- Player settings are stored PER SAVE SLOT, never globally: they live in the slot's
  `[options]` section (res://scripts/save_game.gd, reached through
  res://scripts/game_settings.gd). Loading a save brings its settings back; New Game
  starts on the defaults, and every future setting must follow this rule too.
- Add a new setting by adding a key const plus a typed getter/setter to GameSettings and
  storing it via option() / set_option(). Do not write a global settings file, and do
  not put a setting in project.godot.
- Changing a setting is not progress: it must not touch the slot's "updated" stamp or
  otherwise reorder the Load list.
- Input bindings follow the same rule as any other setting: the slot's `[bindings]`
  section (see Input and controls), never a global bindings file.

## Collision layers
- Layer 1 is the world and the player: ground, structures, props and the player body.
- Layer 2 is actors - NPCs, monsters and animals (npc.tscn, villager.tscn, goblin.tscn,
  sheep.tscn). They use mask 1|2, so they still collide with the world, with each other
  and with the player. Never move a structure onto layer 2.
- The player's mask is 1|2, so actors block it and cannot be walked through. Only the dash
  drops the actor bit, for the length of its burst - which is what lets a dash pass through
  a person or a creature, but never through a wall, floor or building.
